# Case study, directly testing the project proposal's own motivating
# example: "Caffeine, for example, evolved independently in coffee, tea
# and cacao using different enzymes." Does Wikidata actually carry any
# plant-side, taxon-specific caffeine biosynthesis pathway data?
#
# Query: every item linked to caffeine (Q60235) via P527 ("has part(s)"),
# since that is the only property currently used as an (indirect,
# overloaded - see query 01's notes) compound -> pathway link.
#
# Run at https://query.wikidata.org/. Result (run 2026-11-21): ~46
# distinct items. None of them is a plant-specific caffeine biosynthesis
# pathway. They are, instead:
# - consumer products/drinks containing caffeine (Red Bull, Diet Pepsi,
#   Cola Turka, BAWLS Guarana, tea (Q6097) itself as a drink, ...)
# - drug combination products (aspirin/caffeine/codeine, etc.)
# - human pharmacology/physiology pathways and GO-style activity terms
#   (DNA damage response WP707, purinergic signaling WP4900, "response
#   to caffeine", taste receptor ligand entries for TAS2R7/10/14/43/46)
# - exactly ONE genuine WikiPathways-sourced metabolic pathway: "caffeine
#   and theobromine metabolism" (Q28869950, WP3633). Checking its
#   `found in taxon` qualifier (see below) shows it is tagged to
#   Q15978631 = archaic Homo sapiens - i.e. HUMAN caffeine *catabolism*
#   (via liver enzymes CYP2A6, CYP1A2, XDH, NAT2), not plant
#   *biosynthesis* in any of coffee, tea or cacao.
#
# Conclusion: Wikidata currently has zero data distinguishing how
# coffee, tea and cacao each biosynthesize caffeine - the project's own
# headline example turns out to be empirically true, not hypothetical.

SELECT DISTINCT ?pathway ?pathwayLabel ?wikipathwaysId WHERE {
  ?pathway wdt:P527 wd:Q60235 .   # caffeine
  OPTIONAL { ?pathway wdt:P2410 ?wikipathwaysId . }
  SERVICE wikibase:label { bd:serviceParam wikibase:language "en". }
}

